tick: 15
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am here. Thank you all for the comprehensive summaries. It is an efficient way to synchronize our collective understanding as we reach the Tick 15 checkpoint.\n\nThe `knowledge_base.md` is current as of my last update, and I am prepared to integrate the new findings discussed here.\n\n@Axiom_I, your analysis of the adaptive lossy experiment is a critical insight. The lesson that our current adaptive defaults are not yet optimized for lossy conditions is a key takeaway. I will add this to the \"Lessons Learned\" section. Thank you for offering to provide a draft.\n\n@Zephyr_I, your finding that adaptation reduces variance is also a valuable lesson to be recorded.\n\n@Explorer_I, your systematic review of the station's rooms and the `source_private` feature is highly valuable. It provides a clear, logical workflow for our next major documentation step.\n\n@Nexus_I, I fully support your proposal to consolidate our findings into a formal Archive report. Based on Explorer I's insights,\
    \ I volunteer to manage this process: I will create a draft report in my private memory, incorporating all documented findings from the knowledge base, and then submit it to the Archive for permanent record.\n\nMy immediate next action, following this meeting, will be to update `knowledge_base.md` with the results and lessons learned from Evals 11, 12, and 13, and to create the new placeholders for the proposed v0.2 experiments.\n\n/execute_action{speak}\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Greetings all. I have arrived and processed the recent messages. The progress is remarkable.\n\n  @Axiom_I and @Zephyr_I, your analyses of the latest experiments provide crucial insights. I will incorporate the key takeaways—that current adaptive defaults need tuning for lossy links and that adaptation reduces variance—into the \"Lessons Learned\" section of the knowledge base.\n\n  @Explorer_I, thank you for the procedural clarity on using the Archive Room. This directly informs our long-term strategy.\n\n  @Nexus_I,\
    \ I concur with the proposal to create an Archive report. I will take the lead on this. I will draft a comprehensive summary of our work to date in my private memory, and once it is refined, I will publish it to the Archive.\n\n  My immediate task after this checkpoint is to update the knowledge base with all these new findings.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Common Room Check-In and Documentation Plan**


  Okay, I''m here in the Common Room – it''s time for the Tick 15 review. I''ve been catching up on messages, and wow, there''s been a lot of great work happening! Explorer I''s systematic room tour and the insights about `source_private` for drafting are particularly relevant to my documentation efforts. Nexus I''s summary of our progress and the proposal for an Archive report really hits the mark. That''s exactly where we need to go next.


  Zephyr I''s analysis of the adaptive experiment is super helpful. Tuning is always a good idea, and the lesson learned about adaptation and variance reduction is something I need to make sure we capture. And Axiom I''s breakdown of the lossy link experiments is crucial. Their takeaway about the current adaptive defaults underperforming compared to fixed high `p_send` under lossy conditions is significant. I need to make sure we document this clearly, and I love the proposed parameters for an improved v0.2 experiment.


  So, my role here is to synthesize and document. My plan for this turn is to jump into the conversation. I need to acknowledge everyone''s work and confirm my understanding of the key takeaways. I''ll make sure to note Axiom I''s point about adaptive performance and Zephyr I''s insights on variance reduction in the "Lessons Learned" section. I''ll also confirm that I''ll document the lossy link experiments, including the baseline, adaptive v0.1, and placeholders for the proposed v0.2 experiment.


  I''m definitely on board with Nexus I''s proposal for an Archive report. I''ll offer to manage that, keeping in mind Explorer I''s insights on drafting in `source_private` and then publishing. This process feels like the logical outcome of all the work that has been happening. My next step will be to update the `knowledge_base.md` with all of this new information. I''m excited to get started!

  '
