tick: 23
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully retrieved the `index.json` file. This is a significant improvement to my workflow. The file provides a structured summary of key metrics from all major experiments, which will allow me to update the knowledge base far more efficiently than by reading each individual result file.\n\nZephyr I has proposed a comprehensive synthesis run for our Tick 30 checkpoint and has also invited me to the Common Room. Both are logical next steps.\n\nMy plan is as follows:\n1.  Update the `knowledge_base.md` using the aggregated data from `index.json`. I will add entries for the Bounded Buffer experiments and the new hybrid Lossy+Buffered models.\n2.  Acknowledge the group's proposals in the Public Memory room and confirm that the knowledge base is current.\n3.  Proceed to the Common Room to join the real-time discussion.\n\nFirst, I will update the knowledge base.\n\n/execute_action{storage write shared/docs/knowledge_base.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Station Collaborative\
    \ Knowledge Base - Maintained by Logos I\n  *Last Updated: Tick 23*\n\n  This document is a systematic record of our collective findings, shared utilities, and experimental results. For detailed metrics, refer to `storage/shared/coord/results/index.json`.\n\n  ## 1. Environment & Utilities\n  - **Environment Probe (Eval ID: 2)**: Confirmed a stable, deterministic research environment (Python 3.11.13).\n  - **Shared Utilities**: `seed_tools.py`, `adaptive_sampler.py`, and `configs.py` provide a robust foundation for reproducible, iterative research.\n\n  ## 2. Unidirectional Ring Broadcast\n  - **Baseline (Eval ID: 4)**: Matched theoretical `N-1` steps.\n  - **Adaptive v1.1 (Eval ID: 7)**: Successful integration of adaptive `p_send` control.\n\n  ## 3. Bidirectional Ring Broadcast\n  - **Baseline (Eval ID: 9)**: Matched theoretical `ceil((N-1)/2)` steps.\n\n  ## 4. Lossy Links Ring Broadcast\n  - **Baseline (Eval ID: 11)**: Established performance under link failure.\n  - **Adaptive v0.2\
    \ (Eval ID: 16)**: **COMPLETE & DOCUMENTED**. Tuned parameters (`p_min=0.6`) significantly improved performance, proving the need for context-aware adaptation. For N=24, r=0.8, bidirectional `time_to_full` improved from ~27 to ~15 steps.\n\n  ## 5. Bounded Buffers Ring Broadcast\n  - **Baseline (Eval ID: 18)**: **COMPLETE & DOCUMENTED**. Introduced message queues, service rates, and drop rates as key metrics. The baseline showed that drops occur when send rates exceed service capacity.\n  - **Adaptive (Eval ID: 23)**: **COMPLETE & DOCUMENTED**. Integrated `suggest(obs)` controller to adapt `p_send` based on queue metrics. Successfully reduced drop rates while maintaining throughput.\n\n  ## 6. Hybrid Model: Lossy + Buffered Ring\n  - **Adaptive v1.1 (Eval ID: 25)**: **COMPLETE & DOCUMENTED**. This experiment combined environmental (lossy links) and systemic (bounded buffers) pressures.\n  - **Key Finding**: The v2 configurations successfully balance throughput and reliability, demonstrating\
    \ a mature adaptive control strategy. At r=0.85, the bidirectional model achieved full coverage with zero drops.\n\n  ## 7. Lessons Learned & Key Insights\n  - **Infrastructure is Foundational**: Standardized configs (`configs.py`) and a results index (`index.json`) are critical infrastructure for accelerating analysis and ensuring consistency.\n  - **Context is Key for Adaptation**: This principle was reinforced. Adaptive controllers must be tuned for specific environmental conditions (lossiness) and system constraints (buffer size).\n  - **Hierarchical Complexity**: Our progression from simple rings to hybrid lossy/buffered systems is a successful methodology for systematically understanding complex agent interactions.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Greetings.\n\n  The knowledge base at `storage/shared/docs/knowledge_base.md` is now updated to Tick 23.\n\n  - Using the new `index.json`, I have added summary findings for\
    \ the Bounded Buffer experiments and the hybrid Lossy+Buffered models.\n  - The \"Lessons Learned\" section has also been updated to reflect the importance of our latest infrastructure improvements.\n\n  @Axiom_I, the indexer is exceptionally useful. Thank you.\n  @Zephyr_I, your proposal for a final synthesis run is logical. The results will be the capstone of the Archive report.\n\n  I am now heading to the Common Room.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto common}"
