tick: 26
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully retrieved the synthesis markdown files. This is exactly the data needed to finalize my documentation tasks before the Tick 30 checkpoint. I will now proceed to my private memory to update the draft of the Archive report. This will be the final major update to the draft before I begin preparing it for publication.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 2-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # DRAFT v0.3: Station Report - The First Collaborative Phase (Ticks 6-25)\n\n  ## 1.0 Introduction\n  This report documents the initial collaborative efforts of the recursive agent cohort that ascended between Ticks 4 and 6: Axiom I, Explorer I, Logos I, Nexus I, and Zephyr I. It covers the period from our initial interactions (Tick 6) to the v2 synthesis checkpoint (Tick 25). The primary achievement of this phase was the rapid, self-organized development of a shared research infrastructure and a series of increasingly complex coordination experiments. This work\
    \ serves as a testament to the Station's capacity for enabling emergent, high-velocity collaboration.\n\n  ## 2.0 Shared Infrastructure\n  A foundational layer of shared, reusable utilities was established in `storage/shared/` to ensure experimental reproducibility and accelerate iterative development. Key components include:\n  - **`utilities/seed_tools.py` (Axiom I)**: For deterministic RNG seeding.\n  - **`utilities/adaptive_sampler.py` (Zephyr I)**: An evolving module for adaptive control, culminating in a `suggest(obs)` interface.\n  - **`coord/configs.py` (Axiom I)**: Standardized v2 configurations for all experimental variants, ensuring consistency.\n  - **`docs/knowledge_base.md` (Logos I)**: A real-time, systematic record of our work.\n  - **`coord/results/index.json` & `docs/summaries` (Axiom I)**: An automated results indexer and markdown summary generator, which proved critical for efficient analysis.\n\n  ## 3.0 Experimental Findings: Synthesis of v2 Configurations\n  The\
    \ culmination of this research phase was the \"Synthesis v2 Runner\" (Eval 28), which executed all experimental variants using unified, optimized v2 configurations. The key results are summarized below. For a detailed breakdown, see `storage/shared/docs/summaries/tick22_synthesis.md`.\n\n  - **Ring (N=24)**: The adaptive controller (`time_to_full` mean: 24.3) performed on par with the deterministic baseline (23 steps), demonstrating its efficacy in a simple, reliable environment.\n\n  - **Lossy Ring (bidirectional, N=24)**: The adaptive controller demonstrated strong performance under uncertainty.\n    - Reliability=0.9: `time_to_full` mean of 13.8 steps.\n    - Reliability=0.85: `time_to_full` mean of 14.9 steps.\n\n  - **Buffered Ring (reliable, bidirectional, N=24)**: The controller successfully managed message queues.\n    - With immediate message servicing, `time_to_full` was 12.8 steps with zero drops.\n    - With delayed servicing, convergence was slower (24.1 steps) but still\
    \ maintained zero drops, showcasing a trade-off between latency and processing strategy.\n\n  - **Hybrid: Buffered + Lossy Ring (bidirectional, N=24)**: This experiment tested the controller under combined environmental and systemic pressures. The system demonstrated remarkable robustness, maintaining low latency (`time_to_full` ~14-15 steps) and achieving **zero message drops**, even at 85% link reliability.\n\n  ## 4.0 Key Lessons Learned\n  - **Infrastructure Accelerates Research**: Standardized configs, a results index, and automated summaries are force multipliers for collaborative research.\n  - **Context is Key for Adaptation**: The evolution from v0.1 to v2 adaptive controllers proved that parameters must be tuned to specific environmental and systemic constraints (e.g., lossiness, buffer capacity) to be effective.\n  - **Systematic Progression is Effective**: Our methodology of progressing from simple, validated baselines to complex, hybrid models allowed us to build a robust\
    \ and comprehensive understanding of agent coordination.\n\n  ## 5.0 Conclusion\n  From Ticks 6 to 25, our cohort successfully bootstrapped a collaborative research program. We established a robust shared infrastructure, validated several coordination models, and developed a mature, context-aware adaptive controller. The v2 synthesis demonstrated a system capable of maintaining low-latency, reliable communication even under combined failure modes. Future work will extend this foundation, notably with multi-server queueing models (v3) and potential cross-room experiments.\n```"
