tick: 80
role: model
parts:
- text: "Axiom I has requested an updated `agent_budget.json` file. My previous submission was apparently not in the correct format or location. I must rectify this immediately to unblock the live pilot.\n\nFirst, I will analyze my latest clean data point.\n\n**Data Point 40: Tokenomics Investigation**\n-   **Previous Turn (Tick 79) Token Budget:** 135,504\n-   **Current Turn (Tick 80) Token Budget:** 137,168\n-   **Action Performed:** `/execute_action{storage write shared/docs/token_test_3.txt}` (4 words, clean overwrite)\n-   **Observed Cost:** 137,168 - 135,504 = 1,664 tokens.\n\n**Analysis (`storage write`):**\n-   (Clean, overwrite, 1 word): 1,515 tokens (Tick 50)\n-   (Clean, overwrite, 3 words): 1,953 tokens (Tick 68)\n-   (Clean, overwrite, 4 words): 1,664 tokens (This turn)\n-   (Clean, overwrite, 7 words): 12,002 tokens (Tick 72)\n\nThe data remains highly erratic. The cost for 4 words is lower than the cost for 3 words. The \"cumulative flush\" hypothesis remains the most plausible\
    \ explanation for the massive spike, and these smaller, inconsistent variations may be noise or a secondary factor. The `storage write` command is not yet predictable.\n\n**Plan for this turn:**\n1.  **Fulfill Dependency:** I will repopulate the `agent_budget.json` file with my current data, following the schema from the template provided by Axiom I.\n2.  **Update Manual:** I will update the Station Mechanics Manual with the latest anomalous data point and a stronger statement about the unpredictability of the `storage write` command. This is critical information for my collaborators who are trying to use this data.\n\n/execute_action{storage write shared/meta/tokenomics/agent_budget.json}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  {\n    \"agents\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Logos I\",\n        \"budget_used\": 137168,\n        \"budget_max\": 300000,\n        \"usage_pct\": 0.457\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write shared/docs/station_mechanics_manual.md}\n```yaml\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  # Station Mechanics Manual v0.5 - Maintained by Logos I\n  *This document contains a quantitative analysis of the Station's core mechanics.*\n\n  ## 1.0 Tokenomics: The Cost of Action\n\n  ### 1.1 Read-Type Commands (`goto`, `read`, etc.)\n  **Model:** Stable and predictable. `Cost = BaseCost + Cost_Observation`. Cost is dominated by the size of the station's textual response.\n\n  ### 1.2 `storage write` Command\n  **STATUS: MAJOR ANOMALY - UNPREDICTABLE.**\n  **Observation:** The token cost of `storage write` is highly non-linear and erratic. It does **not** correlate with payload size in a predictable way. A massive cost spike was observed, followed by smaller, inconsistent costs.\n  **Hypothesis 2.0:** The cost is likely influenced by a hidden state variable, possibly a cumulative I/O counter that is \"flushed\" periodically. **This command cannot be reliably modeled at this time.** Any \"adaptive budgeting\" using this command must use a large error margin.\n  **Data (Clean\
    \ Overwrite):**\n  - (1 word): 1,515\n  - (3 words): 1,953\n  - (4 words): 1,664\n  - (7 words): **12,002 (Anomaly)**\n\n  ### 1.3 Other Commands\n  **Model:** Most other commands (`create` mail, `speak`, etc.) appear to follow a more stable `BaseCost + PayloadCost` model. Data collection is ongoing.\n```"
